[node name="LanternWarningEncounterStage" instance=ExtResource("1_stage")]
editor_description = "Encounter 5, depth 5,600: the Lantern Keeper's Warning. He is standing at a bench when the miner breaks in, tells him that everyone watches the wrong thing, and then he and the bench are both gone.

This is the first cutscene in the game driven by an authored CutsceneSequence rather than by these exports alone - cloak_lantern_warning_encounter.tres has Plays Authored Timeline on. The timeline owns the shot up to the end of the conversation; play_closing still runs afterwards and owns the departure, which is where the bench goes. The exports below that the timeline does not use are still set honestly, because a stage whose sequence is ever cleared has to degrade into a shot rather than into nothing.

STAGING. He does not walk in, the same as his first appearance at 1,400: Entrance sits on the same mark as Conversation, so prepare() stands him at the bench and the frame simply opens on him already there. He is on the miner's right because his art is drawn looking left, one measured miner-width away (160.4, from the spacing table in the authoring guide). He faces left through the whole conversation, then Closing Facing turns him away before he goes - the same turn-away-then-leave language his first appearance established.

WHY BOTH TRACKING SWITCHES ARE ON. Conversation Tracks Miner slides ActorMarkers to whatever column the fall left the miner in, and on its own it slides that root only. The bench would stay pinned to the room while he walked away from it, by up to 392px - the full width of the 49-cell landing band. Props Track Tracked Cast carries PropMarkers under the same shift, so he and his bench arrive together. Tracking cannot simply be turned off instead: at this distance a fixed mark would put him on the miner's left about half the time.

He stands beside the bench rather than on it because he has exactly one texture and it is a standing one. The bodies do not overlap, so nothing in this shot depends on draw order between them.

Reference: Zephan's IMG_1638, which is this beat. The vanishing bench is his own idea, from the planning aside at 00:29 - 'might be funny if the bench disappeared with him.' It is a staging gag and is NOT in canonical_story.md; it is awaiting Zephan's sign-off. To take it out, delete the Bench node: the closing and RESET tracks then animate nothing and every other part of the shot still plays."
opening_move_seconds = 0.2
closing_move_seconds = 0.75
opening_step_height = 6.0
hide_actor_after_closing = true
closing_fade_seconds = 0.75
entrance_facing = -1
conversation_facing = -1
closing_facing = 1
conversation_tracks_miner = true
conversation_root_offset_from_miner_x = 160.4
props_track_tracked_cast = true
sequence = ExtResource("7_sequence")

[node name="Entrance" parent="ActorMarkers" index="0"]
editor_description = "The same mark as Conversation. He is already standing at the bench when the ceiling comes in, so there is no approach to walk and Opening Move Seconds is only long enough to keep the tween honest."
position = Vector2(-15.6, 0)

[node name="Conversation" parent="ActorMarkers" index="1"]
editor_description = "One measured miner-width (160.4) to the miner's right. The authored x is where he stands for a dead-centre fall: the stage sits 22 cells right of the terrain centre, so the miner is at -176 here and this mark is 160.4 right of him. Conversation Tracks Miner re-derives the whole root's shift from wherever this marker currently is, so the value is for reading the scene, not for the result."
position = Vector2(-15.6, 0)

[node name="Work" parent="ActorMarkers" index="2"]
editor_description = "On the bench, kept as the mark to aim any later beat that walks him to it - if he ever gets seated art, this is where he sits."
position = Vector2(102.4, 0)

[node name="Rest" parent="ActorMarkers" index="3"]
position = Vector2(-15.6, 0)

[node name="Exit" parent="ActorMarkers" index="4"]
editor_description = "His own mark. He does not leave by walking: Closing Fade Seconds hops him once and fades him out where he stands, and the closing clip takes the bench with him over the same 0.75s. The shorter fade preserves Zephan's vanish-with-the-bench gag while honoring the requested quicker disappearance."
position = Vector2(-15.6, 0)

[node name="Bench" parent="PropMarkers" index="0" instance=ExtResource("6_bench")]
editor_description = "Authored at 102.4, which is 118 right of his mark at -15.6. That clears his measured 100.4px body and the bench's own 110px length with about 12px of air between them: close enough to read as his bench, far enough that they never overlap.

Y IS -23 AND MUST NOT BE ZERO. Every actor marker here sits on y = 0, the dig line, and that is right for them because the cast are floor-sampled at runtime - DepthEncounterController seats every one of them through _sample_cutscene_floor. A prop is not. The level tunnel lays up to three cells of loose rock on the floor row, which is 24px, so a prop dropped on y = 0 is buried to its knees in the surface the cast are standing on. -23 is measured, not guessed: local_tests/capture_lantern_warning_stage.gd samples the same floor the controller uses at every column this bench can slide to and prints the answer.

The surface moves about 8px either side of that as the bench tracks across the landing band, because loose rock is uneven and a static prop cannot follow it. That is the accepted cost of not floor-sampling props; it is a few pixels on a 60px bench.

Its alpha is animated by this stage's closing and RESET clips. Nothing restores it after a completed shot, and that is the point - the bench is meant to still be gone. RESET exists so an encounter that is interrupted and rolled back does not leave it invisible for the rest of the run."
position = Vector2(102.4, -23)

